9.4  ext4, XFS, btrfs or ZFS?

TL;DR
  - ext4: the safe default (Debian, Ubuntu). Mature, fast, repairable.
  - XFS: big files and parallel workloads (RHEL's default). Can grow but never
    shrink.
  - btrfs: snapshots, compression, checksums (Fedora's and openSUSE's default).
    ZFS: the same ideas, for storage servers with many disks.
  - For a USB stick shared with Windows and macOS: exFAT.

      lsblk -f                 # filesystem of each partition
      findmnt -t ext4,xfs,btrfs

On your machine
  /         btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /var/lib/my data btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /boot/efi vfat on /dev/nvme0n1p1
  /media/usb exfat on /dev/sda
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2

Pitfalls
  - FAT32 can't hold a file over 4 GiB: big copies fail with an odd error. exFAT
    has no such limit.
  - Linux permissions don't exist on FAT and exFAT: every file gets the same
    owner and rights, set at mount time.
  - Btrfs RAID 5 and 6 still carry warnings: use RAID 1 or 10 profiles, or ZFS,
    for parity RAID.
  - Changing filesystem means reformatting: back up first.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 9.4 --why

Related
  Commands  lsblk, findmnt
  Paths     /etc/fstab
  Hub       atfq disk Disk
  See also  atfq 9.3 Copy-on-write filesystems: snapshots and checksums
  See also  atfq 9.2 Journaling: how a filesystem survives a crash
